{"agent_id":"pp3g-n9vG-review-the-154-durable-opencode-silent-hang-fix-f670","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-04T06:40:39.056554515Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"563a7d84-b3d1-4525-8cfb-7906cf81e9c4","comment_uuid":"57f334c8-4bdb-4a88-a952-3867de514336","author":"pp3g-n9vG-review-the-154-durable-opencode-silent-hang-fix-f670","content":"# REVIEWER VERDICT r1 (#176) — #154 durable silent-hang fix design (#175)\n\n**Verdict: CONDITIONAL** — the diagnosis is sound and most of the design's factual claims are independently verified, but three blockers make the design unsafe to implement as written, and one unreconciled causal gap means the proposed fix may not cover the production failure at all.\n\nMethod: I did not take the design's assertions on trust. I re-derived every checkable claim from (a) the live installed binary `/home/claude-code/.nvm/.../opencode-ai/bin/opencode.exe` (embedded plaintext minified JS) and (b) upstream source downloaded at tags `v1.18.11` / `v1.18.12`.\n\n---\n\n## A. CLAIMS INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED (design is correct on these)\n\n| Design claim | Verification | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sst/opencode` = `anomalyco/opencode` (rename, 301) | `curl -sI https://github.com/sst/opencode` -> `301`, `location: .../anomalyco/opencode`; `api.github.com/repos/anomalyco/opencode` -> `full_name: anomalyco/opencode` | CONFIRMED |\n| `provider.ts` byte-identical v1.18.11 vs v1.18.12 | downloaded both raw files: 2011 lines each, `diff` -> identical | CONFIRMED |\n| Installed binary upgraded to 1.18.12, #145 patch wiped | `opencode --version` = 1.18.12; mtime `Aug 4 03:15`; `if(false)return` occurrences = **0**; original guard occurrences = **2** | CONFIRMED — box is unprotected right now |\n| Hunk base text matches upstream | `provider.ts` L35 `OPENAI_HEADER_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT`, L37 `wrapSSE`, L40 guard, L1738-1767 fetch wrapper — verbatim match to the design's context lines | CONFIRMED, diff will apply |\n| `SessionRetry.policy` is unbounded | `Zd(e){return na.fromStepWithMetadata(...)}`; the only stop is `me.done(o.attempt)` when `da()` (retryable) returns falsy. No `recurs`, no attempt cap | CONFIRMED |\n| `MessageV2.fromError`: `ResponseStreamError` -> `APIError{isRetryable:true}` | `case Q instanceof l0.ResponseStreamError: return new i0({message, isRetryable:!0, metadata:{code:Q.name}})` | CONFIRMED |\n| `halt()` surfaces a visible ERROR | `ze=s.fn(\"SessionProcessor.halt\")(function*(c){yield* s.logError(\"process\",{...})...publish(R.Event.Error)...set({type:\"idle\"})})`, wired as `.retry(wl.policy({...})), s.catch(ze)` | CONFIRMED (mechanism exists) |\n| `autoupdate:false` disables the self-updater | `if(j.autoupdate===!1||T.OPENCODE_DISABLE_AUTOUPDATE)return` before `J.upgrade(...)` | CONFIRMED |\n| Wrapper indirection reaches kickoff agents | `~/.local/bin/claude` sets `OPENCODE=\"/home/claude-code/.local/bin/opencode\"` and `exec systemd-run ... \"$OPENCODE\" run ...`; `~/.local/bin` precedes the nvm bin in `PATH`; wrapper's `REAL=` is the only indirection point | CONFIRMED — the strategy is sound |\n| hy3 F4 (per-request abort controller) | provider.ts:1746 `const chunkAbortCtl = ... ? new AbortController() : undefined` is **inside** the per-call `options[\"fetch\"]` closure; only the SDK instance is memoised (`s.sdk.get(key)`), not the controller | CONFIRMED by source — no test needed to answer it |\n| Build mechanics | `packages/opencode/script/build.ts` L19 `--single` exists; output `dist/<name>/bin/opencode`; `\"build\": \"bun run script/build.ts\"`; bun absent; network OK; 32 GB free | CONFIRMED (with caveats in F11) |\n\nCredit: the fork-naming reconciliation, the retry root-cause localisation, and the §0 upgrade-wipe finding are all correct and materially raise the quality of this design over #145.\n\n---\n\n## B. BLOCKERS (must be resolved before implementation)\n\n### B1 — The patch covers only ONE of TWO shipped implementations of the guard\n\nThe design patches `packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts`. The shipped binary contains **two independent copies** of the SSE wrapper:\n\n- offset `97992597` — fn `dy`, the v1 `packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts` path; raises `ProviderError.ResponseStreamError`; paired with `py()` = headerTimeout.\n- offset `99193357` — fn `or`, inside the `@opencode/v2/AISDK` service. Source located: **`packages/core/src/aisdk.ts`** (235 lines): L26 `function wrapSSE`, L29 the identical content-type guard, L36 `new Error(\"SSE read timed out\")`, L83 `const chunkTimeout = options.chunkTimeout`, L117-118 the same `wrapSSE` dispatch. It is imported by `packages/core/src/session/runner/model.ts` — the v2 SessionRunner.\n\nConsequences the design does not address:\n1. #145's byte patch neutralised **both** copies. The source patch as designed neutralises **one**. As written, the \"durable\" fix is a *coverage regression* relative to the interim patch it supersedes.\n2. `packages/core/src/aisdk.ts` has **no headerTimeout at all** — the one failure mode #144 proved is currently protected is unprotected on that path.\n3. It raises a plain `Error`, which `MessageV2.fromError` routes to `case Q instanceof Error -> UnknownError` — **not** an `APIError`, so `retryable()` returns undefined and §4's whole retry/error analysis does not hold on that path.\n\n**Required fix:** patch both files, or state — with evidence — that the v2 runner is unreachable in our launch mode (`opencode run` via the claude wrapper) and record it as an explicit assumption to re-check on every rebase. Supporting evidence that v1 is the live path for `opencode run`: #144 observed 4 `ProviderHeaderTimeoutError` entries, and only the v1 path can emit those. That is suggestive, not conclusive, and the design must say so.\n\n### B2 — Option B picks the wrong deadline *shape*; the A/B table omits the option that dominates both\n\nThe design frames the choice as \"reuse `chunkTimeout` (per-chunk idle) for all bodies\" vs \"one total-completion deadline for non-SSE\". The third option is missing and is strictly better than both:\n\n**Option A': a distinct, longer per-chunk *idle* deadline for the non-SSE branch** (e.g. `bodyIdleTimeout = 600_000`), i.e. exactly `wrapSSE`'s mechanism with its own, more generous knob.\n\n- hy3 F2 protection is **identical** to Option B. The design's rationale (\"`wrapBody` starts ONE timer and clears it only on `done`; it cannot false-fire on a slow-but-steady body\") is true of an idle timer as well — an idle timer resets on every chunk, so a slow-but-*progressing* body never trips it. The design's §3 table therefore mis-scores Option A': it charges the *idle* mechanism with a false-abort risk that only exists because `chunkTimeout` is tuned for SSE, not because idle timing is the wrong shape.\n- Detection latency is far better. Under Option B with `bodyTimeout=1_800_000` and a 3-attempt budget, a dead body burns up to **30 min per attempt, ~90 min total**, before any ERROR appears. The interim #145 patch it replaces detected the same hang in 300 s. Trading a 10.9 h hang for a 90 min hang is a weak outcome for a fix whose entire purpose is timely detection.\n- Option B introduces a **new false-abort class the design never acknowledges**: a legitimately long *streaming* response that is making steady progress but exceeds 30 min wall-clock is killed mid-generation. This is not hypothetical here — the production failure is a *streaming LLM response arriving with a non-SSE content-type*. `non-SSE content-type` is not a synonym for `non-streaming body`: Bedrock's `application/vnd.amazon.eventstream` (the exact case in upstream #26487), ndjson, and `text/plain` streaming all land in the non-SSE branch. A total-completion deadline is the correct guard only for single-shot bodies, and the dispatcher cannot tell the two apart from content-type alone.\n\n**Required fix:** use an idle-progress deadline on the non-SSE branch (optionally *plus* a very generous absolute cap, e.g. slightly under the 1 h request `timeout`, as a belt-and-braces). Justify the defaults numerically against the configured `timeout: 3600000` and the retry budget so that: idle deadline x attempts < request timeout < operator/kickoff timeout.\n\n### B3 — A blanket `RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3` regresses deliberate rate-limit waiting\n\nVerified in source:\n- `SessionRetry.delay` honours `retry-after-ms` / `retry-after` (seconds or HTTP-date) and caps only at `RETRY_MAX_DELAY = 2147483647` (~24.8 days). The 30 s cap (`RETRY_MAX_DELAY_NO_HEADERS`) applies **only** when no response headers are present.\n- Upstream's own test file `packages/opencode/test/session/retry.test.ts` contains `test(\"uses retry-after values even when exceeding 10 minutes with headers\")` — waiting out long rate-limit windows is *intended* behaviour, not an accident.\n- `retryable()` builds first-class payloads for `FreeUsageLimitError` and `GoUsageLimitError` with messages of the form \"Usage limit reached. It will reset in 3 hours\".\n\nA global 3-attempt cap converts \"wait out the limit and continue\" into \"hard-fail after ~6 s\" for exactly the Zen/Go rate limits this project's own docs describe as opaque and hours-long. That is a serious, self-inflicted availability regression on every long agent run, introduced to fix an unrelated failure mode.\n\n**Required fix:** scope the budget by error class — a tight attempt cap for timeout/stream-class errors (`ResponseStreamError`, `HeaderTimeoutError`, ECONNRESET), and preserve current behaviour (or a wall-clock budget) for 429 / usage-limit responses carrying `retry-after`. A total elapsed-retry-time budget is a better primitive than an attempt count here, because with headers present the delay itself is unbounded.\n\n---\n\n## C. HIGH SEVERITY\n\n### F4 — The cap is not what makes errors visible, and the design never explains why the existing ERROR log didn't fire\n\nTwo error channels already exist upstream and both should have produced operator-visible output in #144's SSE-abort case:\n- `packages/opencode/src/session/llm.ts:283` — `Effect.logError(\"stream error\", {providerID, modelID, session.id, ..., error})` inside `streamText({ onError })`.\n- `SessionRetry.policy`'s `set()` callback publishes session status `{type:\"retry\", attempt, message, action, next}` on **every** attempt.\n\n#144 nonetheless reports zero ERROR entries. Until that discrepancy is explained (log-level filtering? the abort bypassing `streamText.onError`? a grep/measurement artifact?), the design's central claim — that capping retries makes `halt()`'s `logError` visible — is **unproven**, and the same suppression would swallow the new ERROR too. In that case the cap converts a silent infinite retry loop into a *silent session death*: strictly worse, because the agent also loses the retries.\n\n**Required fix:** (a) log at the retry boundary itself — one WARN/ERROR per attempt with attempt number, error code and next-delay. This fixes operator blindness with **zero** policy risk and is independent of B3. (b) Make \"an ERROR line appears in the log file the watcher reads\" an explicit, grep-asserted pass criterion, not an expectation.\n\n### F5 — The causal model may not cover the production hang, so the harness can go green while production still hangs\n\nThe live config sets `timeout: 3600000` (1 h) on both providers, and #145 demonstrated that the request-level `AbortSignal.timeout` **does** fire on a stalled body (observed at 15 s in the harness). Yet the real hang (#142/OZWZ) ran **10.9 h with zero retry POSTs and zero errors**. A raw-body stall bounded by a 1 h request-level abort cannot produce that signature. Either the abort did not propagate, or the stall is above the raw-body layer — note `session/llm.ts:373` consumes the AI SDK's parsed `fullStream` via `Stream.fromAsyncIterable` with **no deadline of any kind**, which is precisely upstream issue #39968 (\"chunkTimeout only watches raw bytes, misses parsed-stream stalls; 9.5 h half-open stream\"), already cited in #145's own upstream-refs comment.\n\nThis is the single most important gap: every test in §6 drives a fake local HTTP server that stalls at the raw-body layer — the one layer the patch instruments. The design has no test and no argument that the *production* stall lives there.\n\n**Required fix:** reconcile before shipping. Minimum: instrument the real path (log content-type/transport and last-progress timestamps at stream start and per parsed event) so the next hang identifies its layer. Better: add a turn-level wall-clock guard above the parsed stream so *any* stalled layer is bounded. Also note the binary contains a third transport (`packages/opencode/src/plugin/openai/ws.ts`, WebSocket Responses with its own `idleTimeout`); it appears OpenAI-plugin-scoped and not on our `opencode-go` path, but the design should say so rather than be silent.\n\n---\n\n## D. MEDIUM — correctness of the proposed `wrapBody` code\n\n- **F6 — backpressure is lost.** `wrapBody` drains the upstream in an eager `async start(ctrl)` loop, ignoring `ctrl.desiredSize`; `start()`'s promise never settles until the body ends, so `pull()` is never used. Every non-SSE body from every provider now buffers at network speed regardless of consumer speed — under the kickoff `MemoryMax=3G` scope that matters. `wrapSSE` gets this right with `pull()`. **Fix:** keep the `pull()` shape and hang one deadline timer off the first pull / stream start, cleared on `done`.\n- **F7 — timer and controller lifecycle are unsound on the timeout path.** After `ctrl.error(err)` fires, `void reader.cancel(err)` settles the pending `read()` with `done`, the loop then calls `ctrl.close()` on an already-errored controller -> `TypeError` -> swallowed by its own `catch` -> `ctrl.error()` (no-op). It \"works\" only by throwing and catching. Additionally `cancel(reason)` never calls `clearTimeout`, and `void reader.cancel(err)` has no rejection handler. **Fix:** a `settled` guard, `clearTimeout` in `cancel()`, `.catch(() => {})` on the cancel promise.\n- **F8 — `bodyTimeout` defaults ON for every provider.** `chunkTimeout` is opt-in (`if (!chunkAbortCtl) return res`); the design's `options[\"bodyTimeout\"] ?? DEFAULT_BODY_TIMEOUT` makes wrapping unconditional, so **every** non-SSE response from **every** provider — including compressed bodies and non-chat traffic (model lists, token counts, auth refresh) — is re-emitted as a new `Response` with `content-encoding` / `content-length` headers copied verbatim. The codebase already ships a `ZlibError -> \"Response decompression failed\"` error class, so double-decode / length-mismatch hazards are not theoretical. **Fix:** make it opt-in (or derive it from `chunkTimeout` being configured), and test a gzip-encoded non-SSE body explicitly.\n- **F9 — off-by-one and a second retry layer.** `meta.attempt > RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS` with 1-based attempts yields 4 POSTs, but §6.4 asserts \"exactly <=3 POSTs\". Also `session/llm.ts:323` sets `maxRetries: input.retries ?? 0` — an independent AI-SDK-level retry layer. Pin `input.retries` in the assertion and state the invariant in POST counts, not attempt indices.\n\n---\n\n## E. MEDIUM — rebuild / replace / rollback\n\n- **F10 — §5.2 is labelled \"atomic\" but is not.** The install step is `cp <dist> ~/.local/opencode-fork/bin/opencode.exe` — a direct overwrite of the live target: non-atomic, and `ETXTBSY`-prone if any process is executing it. The `mv` in §5.2 only relocates the *previous* file. **Fix:** `cp` to `bin/.opencode.exe.new` in the same filesystem, then `mv` (rename) over the target — the exact pattern #145 used successfully.\n- **F11 — build steps will likely fail as written.** `build.ts:26-50` runs `bun run --cwd packages/app build` and embeds the web UI unless `--skip-embed-web-ui` is passed — an entire SolidJS app build the design never mentions in its command list. `bun install` should run at the **workspace root** (root `package.json` declares `workspaces` + a `catalog`, and a root `postinstall` -> `packages/core fix-node-pty`), not inside `packages/opencode`. `packageManager: \"bun@1.3.14\"` should be pinned rather than installing \"latest\" from `bun.sh/install`. Also state whether `--skip-embed-web-ui` is acceptable (it degrades the TUI/web surface the operator uses interactively).\n- **F12 — no fork-identity marker and no drift detection.** A fork build reports `--version 1.18.12`, indistinguishable from stock — which is exactly how the 03:15 upgrade went unnoticed until this design's author found it. `build.ts:192-199` already exposes `OPENCODE_VERSION` and `OPENCODE_CHANNEL` defines, so a marker is nearly free. **Fix:** build with a distinguishable version/channel suffix, and add a launch-time assertion (in `~/.local/bin/opencode`, or in the #146 watcher) that fails loudly when the running binary lacks the patch marker. Upgrade *survival* without upgrade *detection* is how this bug came back.\n- **F13 — no interim mitigation.** §0 correctly states the box is vulnerable right now, then prescribes a multi-step, toolchain-dependent build with no time estimate. Two zero-risk stopgaps are missing and should be step 1 of the implementation issue:\n  1. Set `\"autoupdate\": false` in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` **now** — verified **absent** today, so the next patch release will silently wipe any fix again; the short-circuit is confirmed in the binary.\n  2. Re-apply the #145 same-length byte patch to the 1.18.12 binary — both guard copies are present as plaintext at the shifted offsets `97992597` and `99193357` — restoring protection in minutes while the fork is brought up.\n- Rollback (§5.3) inherits F10's non-atomic `cp`; otherwise the backup-and-flip-`REAL` path is sound.\n\n---\n\n## F. VERIFICATION HARNESS (§6) — sufficient in structure, insufficient in coverage\n\nGood: the reuse plan is real (`/tmp/opencode-hang-test/` and `/tmp/opencode-145-patch/` both still exist on disk, verified), the slow-legit non-SSE test (test 6) is the right test for hy3 F2, and the sensitivity test (test 8) is the right way to prove the knob is the mechanism.\n\nMissing:\n1. **A stalled-but-not-dead stream** — chunks arriving every N minutes with N > idle threshold but total < `bodyTimeout`. This is the test that discriminates Option B from Option A' and would have surfaced B2.\n2. **Compressed (gzip) non-SSE body** and **non-chat requests** through the wrapper (F8).\n3. **Real-provider capture** — every test uses a fake local server, so the production content-type/transport that actually hangs is still unknown (F5). Add a one-off instrumented run against the real provider.\n4. **Unit tests in the fork.** `packages/opencode/test/session/retry.test.ts` already exists and is the natural home for the attempt-cap test; a `wrapBody`/dispatcher unit test in the provider tests is the cheapest thing that protects the divergence across upstream rebases. An end-to-end-only harness will silently rot on the first rebase.\n5. **Test 7 (ctl.abort per-request) is already answered by source** (provider.ts:1746, controller constructed inside the per-call fetch closure; only the SDK instance is cached). Keep it as a regression guard, but state the source proof so the answer does not depend on a timing-sensitive concurrency test.\n6. **Assert the ERROR line by grepping the log file**, and assert POST counts, not attempt indices (F9).\n\n---\n\n## G. VERDICT AND REQUIRED FIXES\n\n**CONDITIONAL.** Do not implement as written. The fork/pin strategy (§1) and the rollback shape are approvable; §2/§3/§4 need rework; §5/§6 need tightening.\n\nRequired before implementation, in priority order:\n1. **B3 + F4 first, and separately from the fork** — per-attempt retry logging (zero risk, fixes the actual operator-blindness root cause) and an error-class-scoped budget. Do not ship a blanket 3-attempt cap.\n2. **F13** — set `autoupdate:false` and re-apply the byte patch today as a stopgap; the durable fork must not be the only thing standing between now and the next hang.\n3. **B1** — patch `packages/core/src/aisdk.ts` too, or prove it unreachable.\n4. **B2** — replace the total-completion deadline with an idle-progress deadline for the non-SSE branch (optionally plus a generous absolute cap); re-derive the defaults against `timeout: 3600000`.\n5. **F5** — reconcile the 1 h `AbortSignal.timeout` vs 10.9 h observation, or add a turn-level wall-clock guard, before declaring the hang fixed.\n6. **F6-F9** — rewrite `wrapBody` on the `pull()` shape with a sound timer/controller lifecycle; make `bodyTimeout` opt-in; fix the attempt/POST-count invariant.\n7. **F10-F12** — true atomic install, corrected build prerequisites, fork-identity marker plus drift detection.\n8. **§6** — add the stalled-but-not-dead, gzip, real-provider and fork unit tests; grep-assert the ERROR line.\n\nReviewer: Builder pp3g-n9vG (#176, independent — no other reviewer sub-issue read). All findings are reproducible from the commands and offsets cited above.","created_at":"2026-08-04T06:40:39.048650116Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:HSE7SGijpZijCJVSfcgbiifYOl9LfT6cnGsBQz/2eIA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgINhBbmb8rdoOOUU0A6fKQ2bIlMPSORkyrJnuMJfZNugAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAr/CEtML+rMAobaXlBSUAKBvyi+CWkevl509vRiaSt/0IiMfPCwiTLNNQXjfUdsR4iQ/WI9Y1i12E80WpqPrjDg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:HSE7SGijpZijCJVSfcgbiifYOl9LfT6cnGsBQz/2eIA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgINhBbmb8rdoOOUU0A6fKQ2bIlMPSORkyrJnuMJfZNugAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQHyvXezfb7vXo2qEuSOX3F0BDv6chdGQpwO3bqRzkZYFCcX+j0eK28FtB+AXPbseU7IffTLpDHNtK175L7jqqAw="}
